            <td>Competitive exam forms (UPSC, SSC, NEET, JEE), state civil service portals, passport renewal forms</td>
            <td>JPG</td>
            <td>600 by 800 pixels</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>100 KB Limit</strong></td>
            <td>US Diversity Visa (DV Lottery), Schengen visa photo uploads, college registration portals</td>
            <td>JPG</td>
            <td>600 by 600 pixels</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>200 KB Limit</strong></td>
            <td>Job application ATS systems (Workday, Greenhouse), scanned PDF certificates, professional ID cards</td>
            <td>JPG or PNG</td>
            <td>1200 by 1600 pixels</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>500 KB Limit</strong></td>
            <td>Real estate MLS listings, LinkedIn profile photos, e-commerce marketplace product listings</td>
            <td>WebP or JPG</td>
            <td>1920 by 1080 pixels</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why Most Online Compressors Overshoot Target Sizes or Blur Photos</h2>
    <p>
      If you have tested other online image size reduction services, you have likely encountered two common failure modes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Overshooting the Ceiling:</strong> Cheap compression scripts compress once at a fixed guess (such as <code>quality = 70</code>). If your photo has high contrast, the output lands at 54 KB, causing immediate rejection by a 50 KB form validator. RemoveSynth enforces a strict mathematical upper bound with a built-in safety margin so your file never overshoots.</li>
      <li><strong>Excessive Blurring:</strong> Ineffective tools blindly compress the whole image down to quality 10 without resizing the dimensions. This destroys text, creates muddy artifacts around facial borders, and makes signatures completely illegible. Our engine prioritizes dimension scaling over excessive quantization, preserving sharp lines and crisp details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy and Zero Server Uploads for Identity Cards, Passports, and Signatures</h2>
    <p>
      The files people compress to specific KB targets are almost invariably highly sensitive: government passport photos, national identity cards, legal signature scans, academic transcripts, and financial verification documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Uploading these documents to conventional online converters exposes you to extreme identity theft risks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Third-party web servers log client IP addresses, session cookies, and uploaded files.</li>
      <li>Many conversion portals cache uploaded documents on cloud storage buckets where they can be scraped or indexed.</li>
      <li>Data breaches on file conversion sites have historically exposed millions of personal ID scans.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      RemoveSynth is built from the ground up on zero-knowledge architecture. All bisection algorithms, image manipulation, and file downloads occur exclusively inside your web browser's local sandbox. Not a single byte of your passport or signature is ever transmitted to our servers or any third-party cloud.
    </p>

    <h2>How to Compress Any Photo to an Exact KB Limit in 3 Steps</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Add Files:</strong> Drag and drop your photos into the box above, or click to browse files from your computer or phone. You can upload up to 30 files for batch processing.</li>
      <li><strong>Set Target KB:</strong> Enter the exact file size you need (such as 50 KB or 20 KB) or click one of the quick preset buttons.</li>
      <li><strong>Save Your File:</strong> Preview the output file size and click download. For batch jobs, click &quot;Download all (ZIP)&quot; to export your files in one click.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Explore Specific Kilobyte Compression Presets</h2>
    <ul class="link-grid">
      {SIZES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/compress-image-to-${s.slug}/`}>Compress to {s.label}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
      <li><a href="/image-converter/">Image Converters</a></li>
      <li><a href="/photo-metadata-remover/">Photo Metadata Remover</a></li>
      <li><a href="/resize-image/">Resize Image</a></li>
    </ul>

    
    <h2>Zero-Knowledge Privacy Architecture and Regulatory Compliance (GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA)</h2>
    <p>
      In modern enterprise environments, data privacy regulations enforce strict protocols on how digital media assets are handled and transmitted across third-party networks:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Local In-Memory Sandboxing:</strong> RemoveSynth operates entirely under a zero-knowledge execution model. Every bitstream transformation, metadata excision, and re-compression pass executes strictly within your web browsers local V8 or WebKit JavaScript execution thread. Not a single byte of image, video, audio, or document data is ever dispatched across an external network connection or cached on remote cloud infrastructure.</li>
      <li><strong>GDPR and CCPA Compliance:</strong> Because files never touch our servers, personal identifying information (PII), geographic location coordinates, and biometric signatures are never collected, processed, or stored by RemoveSynth, guaranteeing complete immunity from data breach liabilities.</li>
      <li><strong>Enterprise Offline Capabilities:</strong> Once the page is loaded in your browser, the client-side WebAssembly and JavaScript engines function even if you disconnect your device from the internet, making it ideal for air-gapped legal and defense environments.</li>
    </ul>


    <Faq items={faqs} heading="Frequently Asked Questions About Compressing to KB" />
  
    <h2>Why Government and University Application Portals Enforce Strict KB Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Online examination forms, civil service recruitment portals (UPSC, SSC, State PSCs), and university admissions systems process millions of applicant submissions simultaneously:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Legacy Database Architecture:</strong> Backend portal databases store candidate photos directly as BLOB (Binary Large Object) columns in SQL tables. To prevent multi-terabyte database bloat, systems enforce strict file size gates (e.g. exactly between 20 KB and 50 KB).</li>
      <li><strong>Automated Document Scanners:</strong> Submitting a photo that is even 1 KB over the limit triggers immediate form rejection with generic error messages.</li>
      <li><strong>The Bisection Search Precision Guarantee:</strong> RemoveSynth uses mathematical bisection search across quantization quality parameters to hit your exact target KB ceiling without sacrificing facial clarity.</li>
    </ul>


  
    <h2>Exam Application Photo Guidelines: UPSC, SSC, IBPS, and State PSCs</h2>
